"} {"question" "q-35734" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040466/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b881bb3817b14744b5b3494377b9605a47e52a1496f2839e9be73e8e2dfdb05" "basis" 1449 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040466/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-4703" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The row echelon form matrix $B$ is given below. What are its pivot columns?\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040466/example-4703.json" "file_sha256" "b4fe1b5b87c6fd10b23c47a4e4044f1efb86729e198230ecb28127902db08fee" "basis" 1449 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040466/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-4703" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $B$ has $3$ pivots in the $2$nd, $3$rd, and $4$th columns:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040466/example-4703.json" "file_sha256" "b4fe1b5b87c6fd10b23c47a4e4044f1efb86729e198230ecb28127902db08fee" "basis" 1449 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040466/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-4704" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The row echelon form matrix $C$ is given below. What are its **non**-pivot columns?\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040466/example-4704.json" "file_sha256" "b73b0d77801282c1979bd221e32df37aa1ea54dd4f324aeabe50f0ae38959dc1" "basis" 1449 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040466/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-4704" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $C$ has $3$ pivots in the $1$st, $3$rd, and $4$th columns:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only non-pivot column is the $2$nd column." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040466/example-4704.json" "file_sha256" "b73b0d77801282c1979bd221e32df37aa1ea54dd4f324aeabe50f0ae38959dc1" "basis" 1449 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040466/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-4705" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the number of pivot columns of the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040466/example-4705.json" "file_sha256" "c2781f4d5cb3175e8cd7d9ba772b78d3609cb1212382f76568e0c75505774f53" "basis" 1449 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040466/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-4705" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to convert $A$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $A$ has $3$ pivots, and therefore the number of pivot columns is $3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040466/example-4705.json" "file_sha256" "c2781f4d5cb3175e8cd7d9ba772b78d3609cb1212382f76568e0c75505774f53" "basis" 1449 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040466/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-4706" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What are the pivot columns of the matrix $B,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040466/example-4706.json" "file_sha256" "7453121ffa8e99ea6b69354d8655afd8007338415748c38e211b6e17a8d40ffd" "basis" 1449 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040466/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-4706" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to convert $B$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+(-\\frac{2}{7}){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $B$ has $3$ pivots. In particular, the pivot columns are the $1$st, $3$rd, and $4$th columns." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040466/example-4706.json" "file_sha256" "7453121ffa8e99ea6b69354d8655afd8007338415748c38e211b6e17a8d40ffd" "basis" 1449 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040466/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-35754" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What are the pivot columns of the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044251/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd54b31305739ec8ae99d7d9b9907b51839e10b2b4c1e1568412700b9e606c11" "basis" 1644 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044251/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-35754" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to convert $A$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $A$ has $2$ pivots. In particular, the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044251/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd54b31305739ec8ae99d7d9b9907b51839e10b2b4c1e